<worksheet xml:id="ws_7_1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>7.1 Practice Worksheet</title>


<introduction>
    <p>
    <url href="https://youtu.be/1GqTc9SbbJ0">Click here for a walkthrough video of some problem below.</url>
    </p>
</introduction>


<exercisegroup>
<title>Transforming Functions</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Determine what function has been transformed and what transformations have been applied to it. Then graph the function.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=x^2+10</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
For the original function <m>f(x)=x^2</m>, then <m>y</m> is <m>f(x)</m> shifted up by <m>10</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=\sqrt[5]{x}-3</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
For the original function <m>f(x)=\sqrt[5]{x} </m>, then <m>y</m> is <m>f(x)</m> shifted down by <m>3</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=\ln(x+3)</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
For the original function <m>f(x)=\ln (x)</m>, then <m>y</m> is <m>f(x)</m> shifted left by <m>3</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=(x-5)^2</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
For the original function <m>f(x)=x^2</m>, then <m>y</m> is <m>f(x)</m> shifted right by <m>5</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=\dfrac{1}{x-7}+4</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
For the original function <m>f(x)=\dfrac{1}{x}</m>, then <m>y</m> is <m>f(x)</m> shifted right <m>7</m> and up <m>4</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=e^{x-0.5}-7</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
For the original function <m>f(x)=e^x</m>, then <m>y</m> is <m>f(x)</m> shifted down <m>7</m> and right <m>0.5</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=(x+6)^2-2</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
For the original function <m>f(x)=x^2</m>, then <m>y</m> is <m>f(x)</m> shifted left <m>6</m> and down <m>2</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=2^{x+3}+8</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
For the original function <m>f(x)=2^x</m>, then <m>y</m> is <m>f(x)</m> shifted up <m>8</m> and left <m>3</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


</exercisegroup>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
What transformations have been applied to the function <m>f(x)=13x^2+2x-4</m> to obtain <m>g(x)=13(x-4)^2+2(x-4)+6</m>?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Because the <m>x</m> in the function <m>f(x)</m> has been replaced with <m>x-4</m> in the function <m>g(x)</m>, this suggests that <m>g(x)</m> is the result of shifting <m>f(x)</m> to the right by <m>4</m>.

And, the <m>-4</m> constant in <m>f(x)</m> is replaced with <m>6</m> in <m>g(x)</m>, which is <m>10</m> higher. Therefore <m>g(x)</m> is <m>f(x)</m> shifted up by <m>10</m>.

In summary, <m>g(x)</m> is <m>f(x)</m> shifted right <m>4</m> and up by <m>10</m>. To confirm, <m>g(x)=f(x-4)+10</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>





<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Below is the graph of a function <m>f(x)</m>. Graph the function <m>g(x)=f(x-4)-3</m>.
</p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="ws_7_1_1">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="ws_7_1_1_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
axis lines=middle,
xmin=-1.5,xmax=6.5,
ymin=-2.5,ymax=4,
xtick={-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,6},
ytick={-2,-1,0,1,2,3},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(f(x)\),
samples=250]
\addplot[domain=-1:2 ,thick] {-abs(x-1)+3};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

</statement>
<solution>
<p>
To obtain <m>g(x)</m>, shift the graph of <m>f(x)</m> <m>4</m> units to the right and down <m>3</m> units. The graph in red is the graph of <m>g(x)</m>.
</p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="ws_7_1_2">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="ws_7_1_2_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
axis lines=middle,
xmin=-1.5,xmax=6.5,
ymin=-2.5,ymax=4,
xtick={-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,6},
ytick={-2,-1,0,1,2,3},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(f(x)\),
samples=250]
\addplot[domain=-1:2 ,thick] {-abs(x-1)+3};
\addplot[domain=3:6 ,thick,red] {-abs(x-5)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Below is the graph of a transformation of the function <m>y=x^2</m>. Write a possible formula for it.
</p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="ws_7_1_3">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="ws_7_1_3_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
axis lines=middle,
xmin=-4,xmax=2,
ymin=-2.5,ymax=6,
xtick={-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2},
ytick={-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5,6},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(f(x)\),
samples=250]
\addplot[domain=-4:2 ,thick] {(x+1)^2-2};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The red function on the graph is the plot of <m>y=x^2</m>. Therefore, the one in blue (i.e., the one we need a formula for) appears to have been shifted left by <m>1</m> and down by <m>2</m>. Therefore, this function is <m>y=(x+1)^2-2</m>.
</p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="ws_7_1_4">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="ws_7_1_4_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
axis lines=middle,
xmin=-4,xmax=2,
ymin=-2.5,ymax=6,
xtick={-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2},
ytick={-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5,6},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(f(x)\),
samples=250]
\addplot[domain=-4:2 ,thick] {(x+1)^2-2};
\addplot[domain=-4:2 ,thick,red] {x^2};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</solution>
</exercise>




<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
A function <m>g(x)</m> is formed by shifting the function <m>f(x)</m> left <m>5</m> and down <m>6</m>. Write a formula for <m>g</m> in terms of <m>f</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Here, <m>g(x)=f(x+5)-6</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
A function <m>g(x)</m> is formed by shifting the function <m>f(x)</m> right <m>2.7</m> and up <m>14</m>. Write a formula for <m>g</m> in terms of <m>f</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Here, <m>g(x)=f(x-2.7)+14</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


</worksheet>